WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.220
Good to have you on SpaceX Daily, Episode 21.

00:00:03.660 --> 00:00:04.980
I'm Patrick in Vancouver.

00:00:05.660 --> 00:00:08.100
Today is July 3, 2026.

00:00:08.840 --> 00:00:12.240
Plenty moving across Starship, Falcon, and Starlink today.

00:00:12.940 --> 00:00:15.600
Starship's six-engine static-fire advances

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:17.620
the 13th flight test campaign

00:00:17.620 --> 00:00:21.320
by validating the full Raptor cluster ahead of orbital attempts.

00:00:22.120 --> 00:00:24.160
The Starship program picked up right

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:26.040
where yesterday's coverage left off

00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:27.580
with today's static-fire.

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:31.160
SpaceX conducted a 60-second static-fire

00:00:31.160 --> 00:00:35.180
of Starship's six Raptor engines at the Massey test site.

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:38.660
The test exercised the complete propulsion cluster

00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:41.460
on the vehicle slated for the next integrated flight.

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:44.660
This step directly follows the prior booster

00:00:44.660 --> 00:00:46.660
and ship static-fire sequence

00:00:46.660 --> 00:00:49.320
and moves the program closer to demonstrating

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:51.160
repeated full-duration burns.

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:55.140
Observers will next watch for FAA clearance

00:00:55.140 --> 00:00:58.720
and the specific timeline for ship 13's launch attempt.

00:00:59.380 --> 00:01:01.780
The Starship vehicle completed a full duration,

00:01:02.260 --> 00:01:05.960
six-engine static-fire lasting 60 seconds at starbase.

00:01:06.620 --> 00:01:09.620
The test exercised the integrated propulsion system

00:01:09.620 --> 00:01:11.220
under flight-like conditions.

00:01:11.980 --> 00:01:15.180
Data from the burn will feed into final vehicle checkout

00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:16.540
for the upcoming mission.

00:01:17.380 --> 00:01:21.140
Community discussion on the R-slash SpaceX subreddit

00:01:21.140 --> 00:01:22.820
centers on whether the next flight

00:01:22.820 --> 00:01:25.440
will attempt new hardware or profile elements

00:01:26.180 --> 00:01:28.420
with users tracking exact timestamps

00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:31.520
from the test video to compare chamber pressure traces.

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:35.840
Several users noted the absence of visible plume anomalies

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:37.520
during the 60-second hold.

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:40.560
Discussion also covers expected changes

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:43.640
to the ship's heat shield tile layout for the next flight.

00:01:44.440 --> 00:01:46.980
Space enthusiasts posted timelapse footage

00:01:46.980 --> 00:01:49.740
of the Starship test stand being reconfigured

00:01:49.740 --> 00:01:51.140
after the static-fire,

00:01:51.740 --> 00:01:54.020
noting the speed of propellant line inspections.

00:01:54.520 --> 00:01:57.460
With Starship's propulsion milestone now in the books,

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:00.740
we turn to the operational side of the constellation

00:02:00.740 --> 00:02:03.420
that already relies on today's launch cadence.

00:02:04.220 --> 00:02:06.720
A Falcon 9 lifted off from Vandenberg

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:10.500
carrying 24 star-link satellites into low-earth orbit.

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:13.180
The mission continued the rapid cadence

00:02:13.180 --> 00:02:15.340
of dedicated star-link deployments

00:02:15.340 --> 00:02:16.760
from the West Coast pad.

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:21.020
Booster recovery followed the standard RTLS profile.

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:24.020
The flight added to the constellation's density

00:02:24.020 --> 00:02:25.700
in the latest orbital shells.

00:02:26.740 --> 00:02:29.820
Amateur radio operators reported successful reception

00:02:29.820 --> 00:02:31.580
of star-linked satellite beacons

00:02:31.580 --> 00:02:33.500
on the latest California launch,

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:36.800
confirming deployment and initial orbit parameters.

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:39.760
Signal strength data is being compiled

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:41.880
for comparison with prior batches.

00:02:42.340 --> 00:02:44.440
Local Florida spotters shared photos

00:02:44.440 --> 00:02:47.420
of the Falcon 9 booster landing zone preparations

00:02:47.420 --> 00:02:48.720
at Cape Canaveral,

00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:50.640
ahead of the holiday week and launch.

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:53.380
One post highlighted the rapid turnaround

00:02:53.380 --> 00:02:56.620
of the transporter erector since the previous mission.

00:02:57.220 --> 00:02:59.000
While new satellites keep arriving,

00:02:59.360 --> 00:03:01.280
others are completing their service life.

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:03.960
Here's what that replacement cycle looks like.

00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:08.320
260 star-linked satellites have re-entered and burned up

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:10.220
in the atmosphere so far this year.

00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:13.860
The d-orbits follow the standard end of life procedure

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:16.980
for satellites that reach the end of their operational window

00:03:16.980 --> 00:03:18.640
or experience anomalies.

00:03:19.340 --> 00:03:21.840
Additional units are already on trajectories

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:24.620
that will produce similar re-entries in coming weeks.

00:03:25.460 --> 00:03:27.100
The numbers reflect the high launch

00:03:27.100 --> 00:03:30.220
and replacement rate required to maintain the constellation.

00:03:31.240 --> 00:03:33.760
Engineers in the R star-linked community

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:35.300
are discussing the implications

00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:37.540
of the reported satellite re-entries

00:03:37.540 --> 00:03:39.700
for constellation replenishment modeling.

00:03:40.480 --> 00:03:43.060
Several threads examine how the d-orbit rate

00:03:43.060 --> 00:03:46.320
affects long-term slot availability in lower shells.

00:03:47.060 --> 00:03:50.240
Maintaining one LEO broadband network at scale

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:51.580
is already complex.

00:03:52.140 --> 00:03:55.500
Now a second operator says it has enough hardware to compete.

00:03:56.280 --> 00:03:59.400
Amazon has manufactured and prepared a sufficient number

00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:02.800
of project kiper satellites to begin initial service.

00:04:03.540 --> 00:04:05.080
The constellation is positioned

00:04:05.080 --> 00:04:07.080
as a direct broadband competitor

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:09.100
in the low-earth orbit market.

00:04:09.720 --> 00:04:12.540
Deployment will rely on multiple launch providers

00:04:12.540 --> 00:04:14.020
for the remaining satellites.

00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:16.720
The milestone marks the first time

00:04:16.720 --> 00:04:19.720
a second large LEO broadband system

00:04:19.720 --> 00:04:21.600
has reached operational scale.

00:04:22.360 --> 00:04:25.520
The development challenges star-links early mover advantage

00:04:25.520 --> 00:04:28.180
in user terminals and ground infrastructure.

00:04:28.780 --> 00:04:32.500
Resolution will depend on launch cadence, spectrum coordination

00:04:33.060 --> 00:04:36.020
and demonstrated subscriber uptake for both systems.

00:04:36.540 --> 00:04:38.840
While two constellations scale on orbit,

00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:41.920
the compute layer that will increasingly rely on them

00:04:41.920 --> 00:04:43.120
is also expanding.

00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:45.940
Here's XAI's mid-year snapshot.

00:04:46.580 --> 00:04:49.740
XAI released its mid-year operational summary,

00:04:50.080 --> 00:04:53.260
covering compute deployment and model development progress.

00:04:54.140 --> 00:04:56.920
The report outlines expansion of training clusters

00:04:56.920 --> 00:05:00.720
and inference capacity tied to existing data center builds.

00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:04.200
Hardware utilization metrics and planned additions

00:05:04.200 --> 00:05:05.920
through year end are included.

00:05:06.500 --> 00:05:08.980
The update provides the first public checkpoint

00:05:08.980 --> 00:05:11.980
since the company's early 2026 announcements.

00:05:12.560 --> 00:05:15.460
The update shows continued scaling of training runs

00:05:15.460 --> 00:05:19.020
and serving infrastructure that will draw on satellite backhaul

00:05:19.020 --> 00:05:20.380
for distributed workloads.

00:05:21.400 --> 00:05:25.020
Power-hungry orbital hardware will need every watt it can harvest.

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:27.720
One startup is pivoting its solar tech

00:05:27.720 --> 00:05:28.880
to meet that demand.

00:05:29.540 --> 00:05:32.760
Veriday Technologies is redirecting its ParovSkyte

00:05:32.760 --> 00:05:36.100
thin-film solar technology from terrestrial rooftops

00:05:36.100 --> 00:05:37.340
to orbital applications.

00:05:38.180 --> 00:05:41.040
The material's low mass and high-specific power

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:44.820
make it a candidate for powering large satellite constellations

00:05:44.820 --> 00:05:46.740
and future orbital data centers.

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:49.620
The company is now pursuing qualification

00:05:49.620 --> 00:05:52.820
for space environments instead of its original ground-based

00:05:52.820 --> 00:05:53.360
market.

00:05:54.020 --> 00:05:56.140
Early prototypes target the power demands

00:05:56.140 --> 00:05:58.160
of high-density satellite buses.

00:05:58.880 --> 00:06:00.340
From an engineering standpoint,

00:06:00.700 --> 00:06:04.120
the 60-second six-engine static fire on Starship

00:06:04.120 --> 00:06:06.980
tests the full Raptor cluster under simultaneous

00:06:06.980 --> 00:06:10.780
high-thrust conditions that single engine or partial cluster

00:06:10.780 --> 00:06:12.220
tests cannot replicate.

00:06:12.760 --> 00:06:16.400
The dominant constraint is maintaining uniform propellant flow

00:06:16.400 --> 00:06:19.060
and chamber pressure across all six engines

00:06:19.060 --> 00:06:22.160
while managing the combined acoustic and thermal loads

00:06:22.160 --> 00:06:23.600
on the thrust structure.

00:06:24.080 --> 00:06:27.360
A successful hold confirms that the propellant manifolds,

00:06:27.960 --> 00:06:31.000
turbo-pump synchronization and gimbal actuators

00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:32.760
can deliver the required impulse

00:06:32.760 --> 00:06:36.180
without inducing destructive pogo or vortex shedding.

00:06:36.720 --> 00:06:39.560
This result lowers the risk that an early cut-off

00:06:39.560 --> 00:06:42.460
or thrust mismatch will occur during the boost phase

00:06:42.460 --> 00:06:43.500
of flight 13.

00:06:44.200 --> 00:06:47.100
The next measurable step is whether the same vehicle

00:06:47.100 --> 00:06:50.600
can repeat the burn profile after minimal refurbishment,

00:06:50.880 --> 00:06:52.960
directly addressing the open question

00:06:52.960 --> 00:06:54.540
of rapid reuse cadence.

00:06:55.180 --> 00:06:59.320
And a quick market note, SPCX at $162

00:06:59.320 --> 00:07:01.540
up 2.6%.

00:07:02.200 --> 00:07:04.900
Before we go, a Falcon 9 launch from Florida

00:07:04.900 --> 00:07:07.180
is scheduled for the 4th of July weekend

00:07:07.180 --> 00:07:10.360
and will be visible along the Volucia County coastline.

00:07:11.040 --> 00:07:14.340
The mission profile follows the standard east coast trajectory

00:07:14.340 --> 00:07:17.080
used for starlink or commercial payloads.

00:07:17.640 --> 00:07:19.520
Local viewing guides have been issued

00:07:19.520 --> 00:07:21.720
for residents east of the launch site.

00:07:22.320 --> 00:07:25.120
The timing aligns with the ongoing high-launch cadence

00:07:25.120 --> 00:07:26.380
from Cape Canaveral.

00:07:27.100 --> 00:07:28.980
That's your SpaceX news for today.

00:07:30.040 --> 00:07:34.580
SPCX is trading at $162, up 2.6%.

